."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-maids-of-honour-tart"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maids_of_honour_tart"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Cheese curds","ingredient/cheese-curds"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"]]},{"h":"More from English cuisine","links":[["Angels on horseback","dish/angels-on-horseback"],["Cabinet pudding","dish/cabinet-pudding"],["Clotted cream","dish/clotted-cream"],["Crème anglaise","dish/creme-anglaise"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Macaroni and cheese","dish/macaroni-and-cheese"],["Manchester tart","dish/manchester-tart"],["Mock turtle soup","dish/mock-turtle-soup"],["Pie and mash","dish/pie-and-mash"],["Pork pie","dish/pork-pie"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Windsor soup","dish/windsor-soup"]]}]},"dish/musakhan":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/musakhan","n":"Musakhan","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Palestinian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Musakhan, also known as muhammar (Arabic: محمّر, lit. 'reddened'), is a Palestinian dish composed of roasted chicken baked with onions, sumac, allspice, saffron, and fried pine nuts served over taboon bread. Originating in the Tulkarm and Jenin area, musakhan is often considered the national dish of Palestine. The dish is particularly popular among Palestinians and Jordanians. It is also eaten by Druze in the Galilee, especially around Iksal and Sandala, and in the Triangle. The dish can be found in Syria, Lebanon, and Jordan as well. The dish owes its name to the practice of Palestinian farmers reheating old taboon bread and make it taste better. Musakhan is traditionally cooked during the olive harvest season."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-musakhan"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musakhan"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Allspice","ingredient/allspice"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Pine nut","ingredient/pine-nut"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"],["Taboon bread","ingredient/taboon-bread"]]},{"h":"More from Palestinian cuisine","links":[["Jibneh Arabieh","dish/jibneh-arabieh"],["Kibbeh","dish/kibbeh"],["Maltout","dish/maltout"],["Maqluba","dish/maqluba"],["Meghli","dish/meghli"],["Ntolmás","dish/ntolmas"],["Qalayet bandora","dish/qalayet-bandora"],["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"],["Stuffed turnips","dish/stuffed-turnips"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"],["Zalabiyeh","dish/zalabiyeh"]]}]},"ingredient/chantilly-cream":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/chantilly-cream","n":"Chantilly cream","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Whipped cream, also known as Chantilly cream or crème Chantilly, is high-fat dairy cream, known as heavy cream, whipping cream or double cream, that is aerated by whisking until it becomes light, fluffy, and capable of holding its shape. This process incorporates air into the cream, creating a semi-solid colloid. It is commonly sweetened with white sugar and often flavored with vanilla. Whipped cream is often served on desserts and hot beverages and used as an ingredient in desserts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-chantilly-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chantilly_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine",[["Sachertorte","dish/sachertorte"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Apricot","ingredient/apricot"],["Apricot jam","ingredient/apricot-jam"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Chocolate cake","ingredient/chocolate-cake"],["Dark chocolate","ingredient/dark-chocolate"],["Ganache","ingredient/ganache"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Jam","ingredient/jam"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"]]}]},"ingredient/kuanfen-noodles":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kuanfen-noodles","n":"Kuanfen noodles","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Kuanfen noodles are flat Chinese noodles made from potatoes. They are commonly used in malatang, hotpot and hot and sour noodles (suanlafen). They can also be made from purple potatoes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kuanfen-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuanfen_noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Sichuan cuisine","cuisine/sichuan-cuisine",[["Suan la fen","dish/suan-la-fen"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"]]}]},"ingredient/soy-milk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/soy-milk","n":"Soy milk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Soy milk (or soymilk), also known as soya milk, is a plant-based milk produced by soaking and grinding soybeans, boiling the mixture, and filtering out remaining particulates. It is a stable emulsion of oil, water, and protein. Its original form is an intermediate product of the manufacture of tofu. Originating in China, it became a common beverage in Europe and North America in the latter half of the 20th century, especially as production techniques were developed to give it a taste and consistency more closely resembling that of dairy milk. Soy milk may be used as a substitute for dairy milk by individuals who are vegan or lactose-intolerant or have a milk allergy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-soy-milk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soy_milk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Tofu","dish/tofu"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Tofu","dish/tofu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"]]}]},"site/en-ceciliatupac-com":{"t":"site","r":"site/en-ceciliatupac-com","n":"Cecilia Tupac: Chifa","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Recipe sites","sites"]],"sub":"Recipe site, en.ceciliatupac.com","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[["Chifa section on the site of Peruvian cook Cecilia Tupac."]],"actions":[{"kind":"out","url":"https://en.ceciliatupac.com","label":"Visit en.ceciliatupac.com"}],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Latin American Chinese cuisine","ref":"cuisine/chinese-latin-american-cuisine","color":"#f0b340","shape":"cuisine","sub":"Cecilia Tupac: Chifa Peruano","url":"https://en.ceciliatupac.com/chifa-peruano"},{"name":"Peruvian-Chinese cuisine","ref":"cuisine/chifa","color":"#94bd5c","shape":"cuisine","sub":"Cecilia Tupac: Chifa","url":"https://en.ceciliatupac.com/chifa-peruano"}]}]},"cookbook/teff-love-adventures-in-vegan-ethiopian-cooking":{"t":"book","r":"cookbook/teff-love-adventures-in-vegan-ethiopian-cooking","n":"Teff Love: Adventures in Vegan Ethiopian Cooking","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Cookbooks","cookbooks"]],"sub":"Cookbook by Kittee Berns, 2015","square":true,"suggested":true,"isbn":"9781570673115","intro":[["Plant-based Ethiopian cookbook whose wots, alichas and injera match the dishes eaten on Orthodox fasting days."]],"actions":[{"kind":"out","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781570673115","label":"Open Library"}],"sections":[{"h":"Cuisines it covers","count":1,"links":[["Ethiopian Orthodox diet","cuisine/fasting-and-abstinence-in-the-ethiopian-orthodox-tewahedo-church"]]}]},"site/indianhealthyrecipes-com":{"t":"site","r":"site/indianhealthyrecipes-com","n":"Swasthi's Recipes","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Recipe sites","sites"]],"sub":"Recipe site, indianhealthyrecipes.com","square":true,"suggested":true,"intro":[[